        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=e^{x} + 3$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int 3 \\left(e^{x} + 3\\right)^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=e^{x} + 3$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(e^{x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{x} + 3>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 3 \\left(e^{x} + 3\\right)^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=3\\left(\\int u^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\int u^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
