        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=e^{2 x} + 4$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(e^{2 x} + 4\\right)^{3} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=e^{2 x} + 4$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 4>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\left(e^{2 x} + 4\\right)^{3} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
