        ":question/id": "234b841b-ddf2-5537-9f3b-c343ab766392",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4 e^{x}}{e^{x} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=e^{x} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(e^{x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nUse a connected real interval on which the integrand is defined.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{4 e^{x}}{e^{x} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=4\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n4\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=4\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n4\\left(\\ln\\left|e^{x} + 1\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
