        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 4 \\sqrt{e^{2 x} + 1} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}+C$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 1>0$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=e^{2 x} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 1>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 4 \\sqrt{e^{2 x} + 1} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=2\\left(\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(e^{2 x} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)+C.\n$$",
