      "question": {
        ":question/id": "11ef6d40-0a09-58bb-8363-01217a52d0ea",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\sqrt{2^{x} + 2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=2 \\sqrt{u}+C$.\n\nWork where $2^{x} + 2>0$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2^{x} + 2$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2^{x} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $2^{x} + 2>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\sqrt{2^{x} + 2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\left(2 \\sqrt{u}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\left(2 \\sqrt{2^{x} + 2}\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
