        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2 e^{2 x} - 1}{- x + e^{2 x} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=- x + e^{2 x} + 3$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x} - 1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nUse a connected real interval on which the integrand is defined.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{2 e^{2 x} - 1}{- x + e^{2 x} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left|- x + e^{2 x} + 3\\right|+C.\n$$",
