        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4 e^{2 x} - 4}{- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x} - 2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nUse a connected real interval on which the integrand is defined.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{4 e^{2 x} - 4}{- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=2\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\ln\\left|- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$",
